WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.500
From its ingenious sound design to a limited
use of gore to Jack Nicholson's iconic portrayal

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.900
of Jack Torrance, The Shining left a permanent
mark on horror.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:12.740
Here's how The Shining changed horror movies
forever...and ever...and ever.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.380
One of the ways The Shining stands out from
the horror crowd is through Stanley Kubrick's

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:18.660
hyper-realistic lens.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.660
This terrifying tale is not told in the familiar
shadowy environment most horror movies prior

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.080
to 1980 had used to bump up the scare factor.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.080
In fact, the majority of the film takes place
in broad daylight.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.600
Add Kubrick's signature style to the mix,
and the result is one beautiful looking movie.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.880
With common tropes out of the way, Kubrick's
signature sense of cinematic framing and lighting

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.390
helps to place the audience firmly into the
mood and world of the Overlook Hotel.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.530
The story becomes accessible in a way that
many horror movies don't.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:48.920
As supernatural as things become, this is
still a human story.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.260
Kubrick nails this point home by presenting
each frame with a heightened sense of realism

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.870
that pushes psychological boundaries in a
way that's almost unnoticeable.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.160
Everything seems fine.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.540
"Little slow tonight, isn't it?

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.480
HAHAHAHA"

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.880
Disrupting this genre formula not only keeps
viewers on their toes, it also results in

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.360
a compelling story that never stops being
creepy.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.020
The Shining made it clear that it was okay
to bring your monster into the light.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.160
Not only does Stanley Kubrick focus on the
get-under-your-skin moments throughout The

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.410
Shining, but he also commits to creeping out
audiences through an unexpected use of sound

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.140
and silence.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.520
As we've been reminded with the box office
success of A Quiet Place, silence can be a

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.540
major tool in amplifying a sense of dread
and danger.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.780
There are moments throughout The Shining — following
Danny's tricycle through the Overlook's winding

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.960
hallways come to mind — where the audience
only hears the sounds of the wheels regularly

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:46.700
transitioning from the hard floor to the soft
carpet.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:00.090
It's not much to watch, but as the camera
follows Danny, our perspective is minimized

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.200
and becomes childlike.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.980
Add that to the unexpected sound cues of the
movie's score, and the results are quite unnerving.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:10.250
The result finds the audience no longer receiving
warnings that the visual horrors are right

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:11.250
around the corner.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.540
And with those guidelines thrown out of whack,
a steady stream of dread and anxiety comes

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.040
into play.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:24.360
“No. ah.”

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:29.700
We've already mentioned the film's color palette
and uncharacteristic use of light and realism

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.520
to add to the terror, but The Shining doesn't
stop there.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.200
What Stanley Kubrick pulled off is akin to
the other classics in his filmography: He

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.300
made a well produced, well-acted, well-written
film.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:43.800
For decades, the horror genre has fought tooth
and nail to climb its way out of the jokey,

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.360
B-movie recesses that have remained its home.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:58.370
At its heart, The Shining is the tale of a
family fractured as its patriarch descends

00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.370
into madness.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:03.069
It's a story that'd be easy enough to tell
under low-budget circumstances, but Kubrick

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:04.190
was a perfectionist.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:07.840
He elevated the genre through cutting edge
methods and high-quality production.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.340
These standards not only left an impact on
horror movies, they forever changed cinema.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:17.020
Throughout The Shining, Kubrick focused more
on the personal demons Jack Torrance and his

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:18.209
son Danny were dealing with.

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:19.880
Yes, there are still spirits haunting the
hotel.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:23.160
But their appearance here coincides with Jack's
own troubled past.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.380
The ghost story is just the mask to the deeper,
more human horrors.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:30.740
Using horror as an allegory for a real-life
issue is as old as the genre itself, but with

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:35.540
The Shining, Kubrick crafted a legitimately
terrifying film that works on all the potential

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:36.040
levels.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.160
If you're scared of hotel ghosts, don't watch
The Shining.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.280
And if a family man giving in to his addiction
in violent ways makes your knickers quake,

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:44.400
don't watch The Shining.

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:49.379
And while prior horror movies like Rosemary's
Baby and Night of the Living Dead were steeped

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:53.870
in allegory, The Shining's cultural impact
influenced modern horror filmmakers to give

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:56.000
their movies depth that goes beyond jump scares.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.459
One of the biggest horror movies in recent
years, Get Out, explores its themes in similar

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:04.380
ways, and director Jordan Peele hasn't been
quiet about drawing from The Shining for that.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.340
He went so far as to admit that he even, quote,
"totally ganked" the opening title credit.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.960
"It's a f------ hedge maze out here."

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:17.820
In the same vein, The Witch director Robert
Eggers told SlashFilm:

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.020
"When I watch The Witch, sometimes I’m a
little disgusted by how much of my film flagrantly

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:23.900
reeks of The Shining.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:27.880
At the same time, I will admit that if it
wasn’t for that, I don’t think the film

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:29.380
would be working for people at all."

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.860
Before The Shining hit theaters, a popular
trope explored in horror was that of "The

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:35.360
Evil Child."

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.539
From the Twilight Zone episode "It's a Good
Life" to films like Village of the Damned

00:04:39.539 --> 00:04:43.650
and The Omen, the creepy child motif had been
a strong one in genre entertainment.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.560
It still is, but The Shining represented the
beginning of a transition.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:51.509
Suddenly, movies started paying less attention
to the young Antichrist wreaking havoc at

00:04:51.509 --> 00:04:55.569
home, and focused more on telling empowered
tales of survival and redemption through the

00:04:55.569 --> 00:04:57.520
innocent perspective of the children themselves.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:01.420
In putting Danny Torrance in a position of
power — with the help of Dick Hallorann

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:06.140
showing him the how to "shine" the boy became
the beacon of hope throughout the entire film.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.620
"You've got the shinning.“

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.380
“You mean Shining...
“shhh do you wanna get sued?"

00:05:11.309 --> 00:05:16.319
As the '80s progressed and films like A Nightmare
on Elm Street, It, and The Goonies arrived,

00:05:16.319 --> 00:05:20.119
the trend of empowered children gaining the
upper hand against the evil adults of the

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.560
world grew exponentially.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.200
Kubrick's The Shining flipped the proverbial
script, turning this adult/child dynamic on

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:26.700
its head.

